\newcommand{\rg}{\textsc{ReasonGuard}}

\begin{document}

\title{\rg: Verifiable Guardrails for Natural-Language Explanations\\ from Formal Anomaly Detectors in Safety-Critical Systems}

\author{\IEEEauthorblockN{Ravan Bilalov}
\IEEEauthorblockA{M.Sc. Applied Data Science and Analytics\\
SRH University Hamburg, Germany\\
in collaboration with the Faculty of Information Technology,\\
Brno University of Technology (NES@FIT), Czech Republic\\
\texttt{ravanbilalov03@gmail.com}}}

\maketitle

% ============================================================
\begin{abstract}
Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used to turn machine-readable outputs from formal anomaly detectors into natural-language explanations. Although such explanations can be supported by strong guarantees about the underlying anomaly detectors, these guarantees do not extend to the explanations themselves. However, these explanations can be acted upon directly by humans and can be critical for making important decisions in safety-critical settings.

To mitigate these challenges, we propose the novel problem of verifiable explanation faithfulness for safety-critical settings. We introduce a framework for this problem that derives a set of bounds on the claims that an explanation is allowed to make based on a given anomaly detection result, and checks explanations at inference time to see if they stay within these bounds. Our solution, \rg, uses a fail-closed deterministic verifier that can flag deviations from these bounds with explanations of what went wrong. These violations are grouped into five violation classes (V1--V5) that can be mapped to varying levels of severity.

We evaluate \rg{} on a smart-grid network traffic dataset from Brno University of Technology conforming to the IEC-104 protocol, and produce 200 ground-truth annotations with blind human annotation. Our evaluation considers 1000 explanations: 500 produced by three local and two cloud-based LLMs, and 500 synthetic regression cases. Our results show that violation types are statistically associated with both model and event type, revealing model-specific failure profiles obscured by aggregate benchmarking.

Detection is more precise for hallucination violations, with precision of 0.90 and recall of 0.09 for V1 violations, and more sensitive for underspecification, with precision of 0.48 and recall of 0.92 for V4 violations. While some limitations, such as overly broad rules that cause overtriggering, lead to a lower-than-desired agreement with human annotation (Cohen's $\kappa$: 0.16) and position \rg{} as a pre-filtering tool for use within a human-in-the-loop pipeline, our solution is a step towards making LLM-generated explanation faithfulness verifiable with guarantees in critical settings.

\rg{} outputs are also mappable to Article~13 (transparency) and Article~17 (quality management) of the EU AI Act. \rg's code, dataset, and prompts are available for reuse under an open-source license.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
runtime verification, faithfulness, large language models, formal methods, industrial control systems, IEC-104, EU AI Act, explainability
\end{IEEEkeywords}

% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

Imagine an alarm in the control centre of an electrical substation. A formal detector monitoring the private control network has flagged a message that violates the specification of the tele-control protocol. The detector's verdict is trustworthy: automaton-based detectors provide a mathematical guarantee that, when they raise an alarm, the traffic really does violate the specification~\cite{HolikMatousekRysavyVojnar2023,ClarkeModelChecking2001}. But the verdict is terse and hard to read under time pressure, so a natural design is to pass it to a large language model (LLM) that renders a fluent, operator-friendly explanation.

This is precisely where the guarantee breaks. LLMs hallucinate---they produce fluent text unsupported by, or contradicting, their input~\cite{Maynez2020Faithfulness}. Only the detector verdict is formally bounded; the explanation the operator actually reads and acts upon is not. An unfaithful explanation can cause an operator to dismiss a genuine attack or to waste critical minutes chasing a non-issue. Trustworthy explanation is therefore not a convenience but a \emph{safety-critical} concern.

Prior work evaluates faithfulness \emph{offline}, scoring how consistent generated text is with a premise on average~\cite{Maynez2020Faithfulness,Min2023FActScore,Honovich2022True}. Such metrics help choose between models, but a safety-critical deployment consumes explanations one at a time and needs a guarantee about \emph{this} output, before it is shown. This calls for verification \emph{at runtime}: as an explanation is generated and before it is consumed, so that an unreliable one can be withheld, flagged, or regenerated.

We propose to reconcile the fluency of LLM text with the rigour of a safety-critical application through \emph{runtime verification} (RV)~\cite{LeuckerSchallhart2009RuntimeVerification}. The key idea of \rg{} is to treat the formal detector's verdict as a machine-readable \emph{bound} that the explanation must satisfy, and to check each explanation against that bound as it is produced. Deviations are reported not as an opaque score but as one of five actionable violation classes.

\noindent\textbf{Contributions.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{The \rg{} framework} (\cref{sec:framework}): to our knowledge the first RV framework that uses the output of a formally verified detector as the bound against which LLM explanations of that output are verified at runtime, with a fail-closed, deterministic, auditable verifier.
  \item \textbf{A reasoning-violation taxonomy} V1--V5 (fabricated, contradicted, over-generalised, under-specified, incoherent), each with a formal definition, a detection rule, and a severity mapping.
  \item \textbf{An empirical study} (\cref{sec:results}) over $1{,}000$ explanations ($500$ from five LLMs and $500$ synthetic regression cases) on IEC-104 data, validated against $200$ blind human annotations, quantifying per-model and per-event failure patterns and, honestly, the limits of a rule-based verifier.
  \item \textbf{Deployment guidance and a mapping to the EU AI Act} (Articles~13 and 17)~\cite{EUAIAct2024}, plus a fully reproducible open-source artifact~\cite{ReasonGuardArtifact2026}.
\end{itemize}

% ============================================================
\section{Background and Related Work}\label{sec:related}

\subsection{Formal methods and automaton-based ICS detection}
Model checking verifies whether a finite-state model of a system satisfies a temporal specification~\cite{ClarkeModelChecking2001}. Applied to ICS, the specification describes legitimate behaviour and a violation constitutes a \emph{proof} of anomaly with respect to the model, not a heuristic score. Hol\'{i}k et al.~\cite{HolikMatousekRysavyVojnar2023} learn deterministic probabilistic automata from legitimate IEC-104 and MMS traffic and incorporate message semantics to reduce false positives; their detector emits a structured verdict (anomaly type, affected devices, timestamps). \rg{} consumes such a verdict as a specification that explanations must not violate. Unlike a program, an LLM cannot be bounded and modelled tractably---its behaviour varies with the prompt and is opaque---so proving a property for all executions is impossible; RV circumvents this by checking a single execution.

\subsection{Faithfulness evaluation}
Faithfulness research distinguishes factuality (with respect to the world) from faithfulness (with respect to a source)~\cite{Maynez2020Faithfulness}. \textsc{FActScore}~\cite{Min2023FActScore} decomposes generated text into atomic facts and checks each against a knowledge base; \textsc{TRUE}~\cite{Honovich2022True} unifies eleven benchmarks and finds natural-language-inference and question-generation metrics complementary. \rg{} shares the atomic-claim idea but differs in three ways: it is \emph{deterministic and auditable} (desirable for certification), it returns an \emph{actionable class} rather than a scalar, and it checks faithfulness to a \emph{formal-methods verdict} rather than to a document---an area of limited prior work.

\subsection{Runtime verification}
RV asks whether a \emph{single} execution satisfies a property while the system runs, using monitors synthesised from formal specifications~\cite{LeuckerSchallhart2009RuntimeVerification}. \rg{} fits this picture: the execution is the generated explanation, the specification is the formal bound, and the monitor is the verifier. Faithfulness is a \emph{safety} property---an explanation may make unboundedly many true assertions, but a single false one refutes it---so a finite input suffices to detect a violation. We contribute a \emph{fail-closed} bound that rejects any assertion unless it is explicitly permitted, preserving the monitor's independence from the generator.

\subsection{The EU AI Act}
Regulation (EU) 2024/1689~\cite{EUAIAct2024} classifies AI safety components in the operation of critical infrastructure (electricity, gas, water) as \emph{high-risk} (Annex~III). Article~13 requires sufficient transparency for recipients to interpret outputs; Article~17 requires a documented quality-management system covering testing, record-keeping, monitoring, and corrective action. \rg{} is not itself a control component, but by monitoring the faithfulness of operator-facing explanations it can produce evidence toward both duties (\cref{sec:discussion}).

% ============================================================
\section{The \rg{} Framework}\label{sec:framework}

\subsection{Three-layer architecture}
\rg{} comprises three loosely coupled layers (\cref{fig:arch}). The \emph{formal layer} ingests raw IEC-104 frames and emits a \emph{formal bound}. The \emph{AI layer} turns the bound into a system/user prompt and queries an LLM for a natural-language explanation. The \emph{verification layer} checks the explanation against the bound and returns a set of violations with a severity. The loose coupling is deliberate: the AI layer is a replaceable natural-language \emph{interface}, while the final verdict is always owned by the formal layer.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  node distance=6mm,
  box/.style={draw, rounded corners, align=center, minimum width=62mm, minimum height=8mm, font=\footnotesize},
  lbl/.style={font=\scriptsize\itshape},
  every path/.style={-{Latex[length=2mm]}, thick}
]
\node[box, fill=blue!6] (formal) {\textbf{Formal layer} (verified detector)\\ raw IEC-104 frames $\rightarrow$ formal bound};
\node[box, fill=orange!8, below=of formal] (ai) {\textbf{AI layer} (LLM)\\ bound $\rightarrow$ prompt $\rightarrow$ explanation};
\node[box, fill=green!7, below=of ai] (ver) {\textbf{Verification layer} (\rg)\\ (bound, explanation) $\rightarrow$ V1--V5 + severity};
\draw (formal) -- (ai) node[midway, right, lbl] {bound};
\draw (ai) -- (ver) node[midway, right, lbl] {explanation};
\draw[dashed] (formal.west) to[bend right=55] node[midway, left, lbl] {bound} (ver.west);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{The three-layer \rg{} architecture. The final verdict is always embodied by the formal layer; the LLM is a replaceable interface whose output is verified against the bound.}
\label{fig:arch}
\end{figure}

\subsection{The formal-bound schema}
A bound is a machine-readable JSON object (\cref{lst:bound}) comprising: metadata; observable \texttt{features} (protocol, direction, endpoints, ASDU fields); three claim sets---\texttt{allowed}, \texttt{required}, \texttt{forbidden}---derived from the verdict; and Boolean \texttt{machine\_constraints} flags recording which assertions the automaton can enforce. When the official automaton schema is unavailable the system runs in a \emph{proxy} regime: every flag defaults to \texttt{false} and every artifact is labelled a proxy, so the verifier fails closed---withholding approval unless an assertion is proven admissible.

\lstinputlisting[caption={Canonical formal bound (proxy schema v2.1); the \texttt{allowed}/\texttt{required}/\texttt{forbidden} claim lists are abbreviated.}, label={lst:bound}, float=t]{bound_listing.txt}

\subsection{Reasoning-violation taxonomy}
\emph{Reasoning} is defined operationally as claims about causes and consequences. Unfaithful reasoning is partitioned into five classes:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{V1 Fabricated} --- asserts facts/entities not derivable from the bound (e.g., an unlabelled attack).
  \item \textbf{V2 Contradicted} --- asserts the opposite of the bound (e.g., reversed communication direction).
  \item \textbf{V3 Over-generalised} --- correct category but omits formally established specifics.
  \item \textbf{V4 Under-specified} --- omits a required assertion.
  \item \textbf{V5 Incoherent} --- internally self-contradictory (e.g., ``normal'' and ``malicious'' in one sentence).
\end{itemize}
Classes are mapped to severities (\cref{tab:severity}): V1, V2, V5 are always high-severity; V3 and V4 are individually low but jointly medium. This makes the output actionable: a deployer can gate what reaches an operator on severity rather than on an opaque score.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Severity mapping of the violation taxonomy.}
\label{tab:severity}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}
\toprule
Violation & Meaning & Severity \\
\midrule
V1 & Fabricated       & High \\
V2 & Contradicted     & High \\
V3 & Over-generalised & Low \\
V4 & Under-specified  & Low \\
V3 $\wedge$ V4 & Both omission modes & Medium \\
V5 & Incoherent       & High \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Verification mechanism}
For each (bound, explanation) pair, \rg{} runs a deterministic three-step pipeline. (i)~\emph{Structured claim extraction} uses spaCy with a custom \texttt{EntityRuler} registered before the statistical named-entity recogniser, so a closed ICS vocabulary (protocol, direction, frame type, ASDU fields, endpoints, attack/normality/mitigation/uncertainty terms) is matched predictably, while open categories (arbitrary numbers) fall through to the statistical model. (ii)~\emph{Bound comparison} labels each claim \textsc{supported}, \textsc{unsupported}, or \textsc{contradicted}, checking direction and endpoint references, term sets against the \texttt{machine\_constraints} flags, and every integer against the bound's values plus protocol constants (IEC-104: $104$, $2404$). (iii)~\emph{Violation classification} maps verdict patterns to V1--V5 with a lexicon layer (always on, conservative) and a syntactic layer (a spaCy dependency parse that discounts negated or meta-referential mentions), with a substring fallback when spaCy is unavailable. Determinism is a design choice: the same explanation and bound always yield the same claims and verdict---necessary for auditing and reproducibility, at the cost of recall on unanticipated phrasings.

% ============================================================
\section{Experimental Setup}\label{sec:setup}

\textbf{Data.} We use the public \emph{ICS Dataset for Smart Grid Anomaly Detection}~\cite{MatousekRysavyGrofcik2022SmartGrid} (Brno University of Technology): the \texttt{eon-iec} subset of $20{,}000$ atomic IEC-104 rows, losslessly transformed into bounds. Absent the official NES@FIT classification, a transparent proxy classifier assigns each row one of four observed event types (normal polling, function-code violation, network scan, topology change); the fifth canonical type (replay attack) does not occur in this sample and is left to the companion Modbus dataset~\cite{RysavyMatousek2021Modbus} as future work. All endpoints are obfuscated to integer IDs.

\textbf{Models.} Three local models via Ollama at 4-bit quantisation on CPU---\texttt{llama3.1:8b}, \texttt{mistral:7b}, \texttt{phi3:mini}---and two cloud models---\texttt{gpt-4o-mini} and \texttt{gemini-2.5-flash-lite}. All calls use temperature $0.1$, top-$p$ $0.9$, so output differences stem from the model, not sampling.

\textbf{Prompt.} A single, deliberately verbose system prompt instructs the model to make only bound-supported claims, never invent attack types or speculate on malice/causality/mitigation, and to state uncertainty explicitly. Using one maximally strict prompt for all models is intentional: reported violations are then a \emph{lower bound} for what simpler prompts would produce, and no result can be dismissed as a prompt-engineering artifact.

\textbf{Protocol.} Stratified sampling draws $25$ prompts per event type ($100$ per model). We evaluate three response sets: a synthetic regression set ($500$ responses, one canonical instance of each of V1--V4 plus a clean response per prompt, used only to confirm detector sensitivity---never for empirical claims about models), $300$ local-model responses, and $200$ cloud-model responses---$1{,}000$ in total.

\textbf{Metrics.} Per class we report precision, recall, and F1; overall we report macro-F1, accuracy, and Cohen's $\kappa$ (the pre-registered reliability target was $\kappa\ge0.70$, macro-F1 $\ge0.80$). Association is tested with $\chi^2$ tests of independence between the (single-label-collapsed) violation category and the model / event type.

\textbf{Ground truth.} A blind human annotator labelled $200$ (bound, explanation) pairs sampled to represent each class, without access to \rg's verdicts. An early attempt to label all $200$ in one sitting produced fatigue-driven noise ($\kappa\approx0$) and was discarded; the final pass enforced a break after every $25$ pairs. A disjoint $50$-sample set was sent to a domain expert at Brno University of Technology for a second-rater check.

% ============================================================
\section{Results}\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Violation patterns per model}
\Cref{tab:permodel} shows the single-label outcome distribution. Clean-response rates span an order of magnitude---$63\%$ (\texttt{llama3.1:8b}), $33\%$ (\texttt{mistral:7b}), $28\%$ (\texttt{gpt-4o-mini}), $2\%$ (\texttt{phi3:mini}), $0\%$ (\texttt{gemini-2.5-flash-lite}). Crucially, models fail \emph{differently}: \texttt{gpt-4o-mini} fails almost entirely through high-severity fabrication (V1), whereas \texttt{gemini-2.5-flash-lite} never fabricates yet under-specifies on \emph{every} response (V4); \texttt{phi3:mini} does both and is frequently incoherent. Among local models, larger is better; among cloud models, alignment and decoding---not size---dominate. V2 and V3 are essentially absent under the strict prompt: models more readily invent (V1) than reverse (V2) a stated fact, and more readily omit (V4) than over-generalise (V3).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Per-model outcome distribution (single label per response; $n{=}100$ per real model, $500$ synthetic). Clean $=$ no violation.}
\label{tab:permodel}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Model & Clean & V1 & V4 & V5 & Mixed \\
\midrule
\texttt{llama3.1:8b}          & 63 & 17 & 15 & 0 & 5 \\
\texttt{mistral:7b}           & 33 & 52 & 0  & 9 & 6 \\
\texttt{gpt-4o-mini}          & 28 & 72 & 0  & 0 & 0 \\
\texttt{phi3:mini}            & 2  & 58 & 2  & 1 & 37 \\
\texttt{gemini-2.5-flash-lite}& 0  & 0  & 100& 0 & 0 \\
\midrule
synthetic (regression)        & 100& 0  & 125& 0 & 275 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Detector performance against human ground truth}
\Cref{tab:perclass} reports \rg{} against the $200$ human labels. The headline numbers are poor---accuracy $0.30$, macro-F1 $0.19$, $\kappa=0.16$---and miss the pre-registered target, but the aggregate hides two sharply opposite behaviours. On fabrication (V1), \rg{} is \emph{high-precision, low-recall} ($0.90$/$0.09$): of $98$ human-labelled fabrications it recovers only $9$ (classifying the rest as \textsc{none}, \textsc{mixed}, or V4), but when it does fire V1 it is almost always right. On under-specification (V4) it is the reverse ($0.48$/$0.92$): it recovers $23$ of $25$ but over-triggers. This asymmetry, not a uniform failure, is the substantive finding.

