\label{tab:perclass}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Class & Precision & Recall & F1 & Support \\
\midrule
NONE & 0.26 & 0.43 & 0.33 & 49 \\
V1   & 0.90 & 0.09 & 0.17 & 98 \\
V2   & 0.00 & 0.00 & 0.00 & 17 \\
V3   & 0.00 & 0.00 & 0.00 & 2  \\
V4   & 0.48 & 0.92 & 0.63 & 25 \\
V5   & --   & --   & --   & 0  \\
\midrule
Macro & 0.27 & 0.24 & 0.19 & 200 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Statistical association}
Two $\chi^2$ tests of independence reject the null at the $0.1\%$ level (\cref{tab:chi2}): the violation category is significantly associated with both the model that produced the response and the proxy event type. The very large model statistic reflects categorical differences in failure mode (e.g., \texttt{gemini} fails only via V4 while \texttt{gpt-4o-mini} fails via V1). The event-type effect is real but non-uniform and model-dependent: no single event type is globally hardest, which refutes a simple ``rare events cause more hallucination'' hypothesis.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{$\chi^2$ tests of independence (violation category vs.\ grouping variable). $\dagger$ five real models; event-type test excludes zero-count classes V2, V3.}
\label{tab:chi2}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrr@{}}
\toprule
Grouping variable & $\chi^2$ & df & $p$ \\
\midrule
Model$^{\dagger}$ ($N{=}500$) & 649.05 & 16 & $<.001$ \\
Proxy event type              & 39.09  & 12 & $<.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Latency}
On an Apple M-series CPU with no GPU, the local pipeline runs at interactive speed, so verification need not depend on a reachable cloud API---valuable precisely in control rooms, where the tool matters most during incidents that may coincide with connectivity loss.

% ============================================================
\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Failure-mode granularity beats scalar scores}
\rg{} lets a deployer choose a model on the \emph{shape} of its errors, not a single benchmark number. \texttt{gemini-2.5-flash-lite} scores $0\%$ clean yet fails \emph{only} through low-severity under-specification; \texttt{gpt-4o-mini} scores a higher $28\%$ yet fails almost entirely through high-severity fabrication. For a safety-critical task, the former may be preferable: its failures are tolerable and correctable, whereas fabrication actively misleads. A scalar quality score would invert this judgement.

\subsection{Interpreting the low agreement}
The $\kappa=0.16$ is disappointing but diagnostic, and it does not mean bound-based verification is unsound---it means individual rule implementations behaved differently than intended. The dominant error is a single V4 rule that fires when \emph{any} of five required references is absent, triggering on $\sim\!55\%$ of explanations versus the annotator's $12.5\%$: the annotator treated V4 as specifically the missing uncertainty/proxy caveat, while the rule demanded all traffic fields. Removing that rule would improve macro-F1 toward the human baseline, but we deliberately \emph{keep and disclose} it rather than tune to the metric. Meanwhile V1's high precision ($0.90$) makes \rg{} a trustworthy \emph{pre-filter}: a human reviewing its output knows that flagged fabrications are almost always genuine (and a lower bound, since many are missed), while flagged under-specification almost never misses a true case but fires readily. \rg{} is thus useful today as the first stage of a human-in-the-loop pipeline, not as a standalone verifier.

\subsection{Mapping to the EU AI Act}
Under Article~13, disclosing \emph{per-class} precision/recall---rather than the flattering aggregate---gives deployers an honest view of where the system is blind (V1 recall $0.09$) and where it over-triggers (V4). Under Article~17, the pipeline's MLflow-tracked runs, versioned artifacts, and re-evaluation-on-update discipline instantiate testing, record-keeping, and monitoring. \rg{} does not by itself satisfy these Articles, but it supplies concrete, auditable evidence toward them---exactly the kind of artifact a formal quality-management process would request.

\subsection{Threats to validity}
All claims are relative to the \emph{proxy} bound; conclusions should be revisited against the official NES@FIT automaton schema. A single annotator labelled the $200$ pairs (a second-rater expert check is in progress). Results cover one protocol (IEC-104), one quantisation (4-bit), and one prompt; the verifier is protocol-agnostic in structure but its natural-language calibrations (V1, V4) would need re-measurement on new data. Per-cell event-type analysis ($25$ responses each) has limited statistical power.

% ============================================================
\section{Limitations and Future Work}\label{sec:future}
The single highest-priority follow-up is replacing the proxy \texttt{formal\_class} with the official automaton schema, after which every artifact can be regenerated deterministically and the evaluation repeated. The rule-based V1--V5 classifier trades recall for auditability; a \emph{learned} per-claim component---trained on the released $200$-pair annotation and compared against the rule baseline via the same MLflow harness---is the natural next step, ideally raising V1 recall without inheriting V4's over-triggering. Broadening to the Modbus, DNP3, and S7 protocols would test generalisation and recover the missing replay-attack event type. Finally, an operator-in-the-loop study is needed to establish that V1--V5 annotations actually improve human decisions---the ultimate safety claim, which agreement with static labels only approximates.

% ============================================================
\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
We presented \rg, a runtime-verification framework that treats a formally verified detector's verdict as a bound and checks LLM explanations of that verdict against it, reporting deviations as five actionable, severity-mapped classes. On IEC-104 smart-grid data across five LLMs, violation classes are statistically associated with both model and event type, and each model carries a distinct failure signature that scalar scores conceal. The rule-based verifier is a high-precision pre-filter for fabrication but not yet an autonomous verifier ($\kappa=0.16$), a negative result we trace to a single rule and report without concealment. In safety-critical settings, an openly admitted shortfall is far safer than an overstated guarantee; \rg{} makes the shortfall explicit, actionable, and auditable, and connects it to concrete EU AI Act obligations. All code, prompts, and data are released for scrutiny and extension~\cite{ReasonGuardArtifact2026}.

